{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'for file in references/behavior.md references/architecture.md references/index.md references/spec.md references/tasks.md assets/behavior-capability.md assets/tasks.md; do cat \"/Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/$file\"; done'","aggregated_output":"Purpose: rules for `behavior/<domain>/<capability>.md`. Template: `assets/behavior-capability.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `capability`, as `<domain>/<capability>`.\n\nTitle: the capability as a verb phrase, such as \"Save an item to the wishlist\".\n\nBody: the claims, in the claim format, 3 to 7 of them, and nothing else. Observable behavior only. Owners live in index.md, history in git, code locations in architecture/boundaries.md, and how it's built in architecture/.\n\nWritten by docs-map when a domain is mapped and by spec-ship when a spec ships, which copies the spec's claims without rewording and applies each Add, Change and Remove. Never edited by hand during a spec; a change to a claim is a Change in the spec that ships it.\nPurpose: rules for `architecture/<area>.md`. Templates: `assets/architecture/<area>.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `area`, the area name. A title, then the area's own table or list. Current state only: no history, no plans. The why links to decision records.\n\nArea files are created the first time they have content, never empty. `stack.md` exists from init, with the commands spec-build and spec-ship run. The folder listing is its own index.\n\n| Area | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `stack` | languages and frameworks, and the commands to install, run, test, lint and build |\n| `conventions` | only the rules agents get wrong without being told: naming, error handling, test style |\n| `boundaries` | modules: name, path, domain, what each may depend on |\n| `data-model` | entities: name, key fields, relations |\n| `contracts` | APIs and events: name, kind, input, output, consumers |\n| `integrations` | external services: purpose, direction, auth, what the product does when it fails |\n| `config` | settings and flags: name, purpose, default, where it's set; never real secret values |\n| `invariants` | non-negotiable rules every spec must keep: ID, rule, why |\n\nWritten by docs-map for what a domain needs, and by spec-ship for the files plan.md lists under Architecture to update at ship.\nPurpose: rules for `index.md`. Template: `assets/index.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `product`, the product name; `docs-format`, the template version the project uses; `issue-tracker`, optional, the address a ticket key is appended to, such as `https://example.atlassian.net/browse/`, deleted when the team has none.\n\nWhat it is: who uses the product and for what, in 2 to 4 sentences, in the user's words. Not a feature list.\n\nDomains: a table with at least one row: domain, what it covers, owner. docs-init asks for the first row. spec-start and docs-map add a row the moment a new domain is named, before any go is asked. The owner is who says go for that domain; on a solo project, that is the user. A domain's behavior lives at `behavior/<domain>/`, so there is no link column.\n\nBehavior: one line pointing at `behavior/`, so an agent that loads only what index.md links to finds the claims. Architecture: one line pointing at `architecture/`.\n\nNothing else: details of any domain belong in behavior/.\nPurpose: section rules for `specs/<id>/spec.md`. Template: `assets/spec.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `id` as YYYY-MM-DD-slug; `kind`, feature or fix; `status`, draft, building or shipped; `summary`, one user-facing line reused in release notes; `request`, the sentence, issue or commits it started from; `issue`, optional, delete the line when there is none. The owner's go is `approved: {by: <owner>, via: <in session, a message link, or a PR>}`, added only when the user reports it, never inferred; git dates it. After a change to Why or What changes, remove the line; the owner's new go writes it back. The go is never a status.\n\nWhy: required. The problem, who has it, and what it costs to leave it, in 2 to 5 lines. No solution.\n\nWhat changes: required. Claims under `### Add · <domain>/<capability>`, `### Change · ...` and `### Remove · ...`, only the headings that apply, one per capability. A Change claim carries a `was:` line with the current text on main. A Remove claim carries a `reason:` line. A fix that changes no behavior writes \"No behavior change.\" and nothing else here. Claims read exactly as they will in behavior/.\n\nNot doing: required for a feature, optional for a fix. What is deliberately out, so scope can't creep silently. Not future ideas with no decision behind them.\n\nOpen questions: while drafting, only questions the owner can answer. Delete the section when empty; the owner can't say go before that. Questions the agent can answer from the code never go here.\n\nA spec past about 80 lines is two specs.\nPurpose: rules for `specs/<id>/tasks.md`. Template: `assets/tasks.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `spec`, the spec id.\n\nAbove the tasks, one line per session, newest last, facts only and never a verdict: `Session YYYY-MM-DD: resumed at Tn; first message: \"<gist of the user's first message>\".` After a change mid-way, one more line: `Rechecked YYYY-MM-DD after: <the change>. T2 redo, T4 new, T5 dropped.`\n\nTasks: `- [ ] T<n> <imperative task> -> <claim IDs, or \"check: <command or observable result>\">`. Each task fits in one session. Tasks cover the build only; shipping is spec-ship's and is never a task. Numbers are never reused. A claim is written as `capability#id` when its bare ID is not unique in the spec. A task is ticked only when the claims it names are proven.\n\nUnder a task, at most two lines: `- note: <blocked, tried, why>` or `- redo: <what changed>`, the task unticked again. A dropped task is removed and named in the recheck line. Anything lasting in a note moves to plan.md or a decision record.\n\nNothing else: no design discussion, no why.\n---\ncapability: \"{{domain}}/{{capability}}\"\n---\n# {{Capability as a verb phrase}}\n\n- {{Actor}} {{does or sees something observable, present tense}} {#{{claim-id}}}\n  e.g. {{a concrete situation}} -> {{the observable result}}\n\n<!-- 3 to 7 claims, observable behavior only (\"keeps one entry\", never \"uses a unique index\").\nNothing else goes in this file: owners live in index.md, history in git,\ncode locations in architecture/boundaries.md. -->\n---\nspec: \"{{spec id}}\"\n---\n<!-- One line per session, above the tasks, newest last. Facts only, never a verdict:\nSession {{YYYY-MM-DD}}: resumed at {{Tn}}; first message: \"{{gist of the user's first message}}\".\nAfter a change mid-way, add one more line:\nRechecked {{YYYY-MM-DD}} after: {{the change}}. {{T2 redo, T4 new, T5 dropped}}. -->\n\n- [ ] T1 {{imperative task}} -> {{claim IDs, or \"check: <command or observable result>\"}}\n\n<!-- Rules:\n- Each task fits in one session. Numbers are never reused.\n- A claim is written as capability#id when its bare ID is not unique in the spec.\n- Under a task, at most two lines:\n    - note: blocked, tried, why\n    - redo: what changed (the task is unticked again)\n- A dropped task is removed and named in the recheck line.\n- Anything lasting in a note moves into plan.md or a decision record. -->\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
